    "storyBody": "Suying had worked below the lighthouse for half a year, mending shades, joining broken rope, and trimming wicks. The winter keeper's post would pay enough to keep her younger brother in school. But the post required climbing to the seventh landing alone. Months earlier she had slipped on the open stair at the third level. She caught the rail, yet ever since, the first gust tightened her knees. Other keepers told her to be brave. Their confidence only made her more careful not to look up.\n\nThe old keeper stopped encouraging her. He handed Suying a small oil can and entered one task in the duty book: second landing, calm weather. She delivered it and wrote the date herself. Two days later she went to the fourth level to replace a pane. On the next attempt, a gust rattled the stair before she reached it. Suying came down without the glass, and the old keeper simply left that line blank. She had evidence of one success and one unfinished climb, not a verdict on the whole tower.\n\nThe work rose by pieces. She carried dry rope to the fifth landing, first with the keeper below, then without him. She practised fastening her tool belt until she could do it with cold fingers. At the sixth landing, she learned to stop against the wall, breathe until she could feel both feet, and decide whether the next step was safe. Every return went into the duty book: height, wind, task completed, and where she had turned back. Oil spots spread across the pages.\n\nA week before appointments were decided, the old keeper offered her an indoor storehouse job. It paid less but required no seventh landing. If she refused and failed the tower test, both posts would be filled by winter. Suying kept the offer overnight, then crossed it out in the morning. Her final task was to inspect the top lamp during an ordinary coastal wind, with the keeper waiting below but forbidden to climb for her.\n\nAt the sixth landing, the wind pulled one fastening loose and her spanner slipped from the belt. It struck the steps twice and fell into the grass. Calling the keeper would end the test; continuing meant completing the repair with the spare tool in the lamp room. Suying pressed the duty book against the wall. The earlier pages showed second, fourth, fifth, and sixth, including the day she had turned back. She closed it, checked the remaining buckle, and climbed the last flight. At the top she tightened the lamp catch and wiped salt from the glass.\n\nSuying received the winter post, though her first month's pay was reduced to replace the lost spanner. In the duty book she wrote 'seventh landing' and the date, then left it open until the ink dried. The wind lifted the corner of the page, revealing the old blank line underneath. She placed the empty spanner loop across it as a paperweight.",
