    "collection": "field-core-fables",
    "storyBody": "Isa could understand nearly every tailoring order in the harbour language. Cloth, cut, size, and colour rarely troubled her, so the master trusted her with the delivery book before festival week. The extra ledger duty paid for her room; another delivery error would return her to unpaid sweeping. One quiet verb ending remained outside her attention: it distinguished work already completed from work still promised. Because the rest of the sentence carried enough meaning, Isa never felt the missing sound. She copied an entry for coats delivered yesterday onto tomorrow's work list, and the cutters stayed late reproducing three garments that were already hanging in the customer's house.\n\nThe master first repeated the whole sentence more loudly. Isa heard the same overall message and made the same omission. He then laid the completed order beside the new one, drew a red thread beneath the only different ending, and read the pair at normal speed. The small sound did not become easy, but it became visible as something to listen for. Isa began marking the delivery column whenever she heard it. For a week she often noticed it only after the customer had gone, and sometimes added it where it did not belong.\n\nDuring the festival rush, a merchant dictated an expensive silk order at the door. Isa understood the fabric and quantity and prepared to write 'deliver tomorrow'. Her pencil touched the page, then stopped: the merchant's verb had carried the light ending. Asking him to repeat would hold up a queue and forfeit the shop's rush fee. Guessing could send a second set of silk clothes to a completed order. Isa called him back. He repeated the phrase and pointed to yesterday's receipt. The shop lost the rush fee, but no silk was cut twice.\n\nAfter that, Isa compared what she had expected to hear with the corrected forms in the ledger. She circled the ending in fast speech, whispered speech, and questions, and began hearing it without a drawn line. Noticing did not make her use it perfectly; tired at closing, she still dropped it from her own sentences. Yet the error now left a place she could inspect. A customer could correct one small form instead of discovering an entire delivery on the wrong day.\n\nAt month's end, Isa removed the red thread from the first pair of orders and wound it around the end of her pencil. The duplicated coats remained crossed out in the book. When the next customer spoke quickly, the pencil paused beside the delivery column before it wrote.",
