    "collection": "field-core-fables",
    "storyBody": "Wenxue promised the harbour newspaper a serial about the lighthouse fire before the winter memorial. She interviewed the keeper trapped above the stair, a child watching from the shore, and the captain of the rescue boat. Each witness gave her only a portion. The keeper remembered smoke and a jammed window; the child saw a bright figure that did not move; the captain watched falling timber and the reef. Wenxue feared readers would become lost, so she braided every fact into one smooth account.\n\nIn her first draft, the keeper saw the rescue boat turn behind the reef, knew why it delayed, and noticed the child on shore. The chapter was tense and wonderfully clear. The keeper read it and placed his finger on the boat: through smoke and a stone wall, he could not have known it was there. Wenxue removed the sentence, but left him thinking about the captain's decision because readers already knew it.\n\nThe captain objected next. From the boat, the tower top had disappeared behind smoke; he had never seen the keeper struggle with the window. The child, meanwhile, did not know the stair was burning and mistook the keeper's stillness for refusal to flee. When Wenxue merged the accounts, every detail might be supported somewhere, yet the mind receiving them belonged to no one inside the night.\n\nShe tried a detached report that described only visible movements. It avoided impossible knowledge but flattened the experience: the jammed window became merely 'a pause', and the captain's dangerous turn became a line on the water. Wenxue then separated the material. The keeper's chapter admitted only what entered the smoke: heat, the window latch, and a distant answering bell. In the captain's chapter, the keeper remained a blurred figure. From the child's window, neither the stair nor the reef existed.\n\nInformation now arrived at different times. Readers waited with the child, calculated with the captain, and discovered the rescue bell with the keeper. The narrator's voice could remain steady across chapters; what changed was whose perception and knowledge controlled each moment. Wenxue pinned three paper windows above her desk so that facts from one account did not leak into another too early.\n\nThe rewrite missed the first serial date. The newspaper gave the opening page to another writer and reduced Wenxue's advance. She kept revising rather than restore the impossible all-seeing version. When the witnesses read the finished story, none claimed it contained the whole night. Each could recognise the limits of the section that belonged near their eyes.\n\nOn the final manuscript, Wenxue drew a small window at the start of every chapter: one facing the stair, one the sea, one the shore. She left the page for a fourth window blank. No wall of the lighthouse could have held a window that looked in all three directions at once.",
