    "collection": "field-core-fables",
    "storyBody": "The new city prepared to commemorate a relocation a century earlier. The official register named the officers who signed orders, merchants who financed the move, and male heads of households. Exhibition designers planned portraits from that list. The people who handled boats, divided food, and watched children had no names in it.\n\nResearcher Anhe found writing on the back of an old ferry ticket: 'Lady Liu made three more crossings and took no payment at night.' No formal report mentioned her. The tax roll listed only her husband, although he had died a year before the relocation.\n\nAnhe kept searching. Flour accounts recorded repeated deliveries to 'the women by the river'. A family letter mentioned someone in a red scarf guiding boats at night. Several elders had heard of Lady Liu from grandparents, but could not say whether she acted alone or whether the red scarf belonged to someone else.\n\nThe exhibition director wanted one named heroine for the poster and offered Anhe another week if she would complete the portrait. Anhe instead traced where absence had entered: officials had recorded household heads, clerks selected orders for permanent storage, flood damage removed ferry logs, later cataloguers described the move through government offices, and the first exhibition draft repeated those choices. The empty place had not appeared at one moment and could not be repaired by inventing one complete life.\n\nAnhe met descendants of river workers before using the family letter and recordings. Some wanted Lady Liu named; others feared one name would swallow the group again. The exhibition sacrificed its heroic poster and added a wall for 'People Not Entered in the Register', displaying the ticket, flour account, letter, recording conditions, and disagreements in oral memory. Labels separated supported links, plausible readings, and what remained unknown, while visitors could contribute copies without surrendering originals.\n\nAt the preview, one official demanded that the empty place be filled or removed. Anhe instead placed a listening bench before it and printed the archive's collection policy beside the wall, showing whose papers it had historically sought. The museum funded new community copying days, but she refused to promise that every missing name would return. Recovery required access, consent, preservation, and time, and some absences would remain.\n\nBefore the public opening, she deliberately left a clearly visible space among the officials' portraits and placed the ticket carrying Lady Liu's name at its centre. Beneath it lay pencils and evidence cards; the first visitor contributed a copied boat roster but left its disputed date visibly uncured.",
