    "storyBody": "During a severe winter, a mountain inn bought an entire store of coal on credit, agreeing to pay after the thaw. Every room stayed full and cash accumulated. The owner recorded only money received and paid, so winter looked exceptionally profitable. He prepared to spend the 'surplus' on a larger wine cellar.\n\nGuest numbers fell in spring, just as the coal merchant arrived with the full bill. After payment, the spring account showed a large loss. The owner blamed weak spring business, forgetting that the money purchased fires already consumed during winter.\n\nThe bookkeeper refused to treat the signed coal order itself as a winter expense. She checked delivery notes, measured what remained in storage, and estimated what the occupied rooms had actually consumed. The used coal belonged against winter service, with an amount payable to the merchant; unused coal remained inventory. When the bill arrived with a disputed delivery, she recorded the supported amount and kept the difference open instead of forcing certainty into the period.\n\nSpring created the opposite temptation. A festival paid deposits for rooms it would use in summer, while several winter guests still owed money. Cash arrived before one service and after another. The bookkeeper recorded a contract liability for the deposit and receivables for earned winter stays, then revised an estimate when one guest defaulted. When coal cash was finally paid, she reduced cash and the payable without charging the whole winter cost to spring again.\n\nAfter adjustment, winter was less profitable than first believed, while spring had not suddenly become disastrous. The owner reduced the cellar plan and began recording long-stay guest charges in the months when the rooms were actually used, even before payment arrived.\n\nThe revised books also changed a practical decision. Once the owner saw that winter rooms had consumed most of their apparent surplus, he cancelled the full cellar, kept cash for the coal payable, and built only a small rack. When a spring debtor failed, the inn reduced its receivable estimate rather than rewriting the winter occupancy. Better timing did not create cash; it prevented one season from borrowing another season's appearance.\n\nWhen the final basket of coal entered the stove the next winter, the bookkeeper checked the delivery note, measured the remaining pile, and wrote first on the winter page. She tied the unpaid invoice to the payable ledger and placed the summer deposit in a different folder. The owner counted cash for wages separately before approving even the small wine rack. The merchant's coins still waited until the snow melted.",
