    "storyBody": "For six weeks, printing apprentice Xiaopu's headache began before noon from Monday to Saturday and vanished on Sunday. The workshop was racing to finish sixty thousand medicine labels, and Xiaopu would lose his production bonus if he left the line. The company doctor, Dr. He, also wanted the owner to renew her clinic contract. Xiaopu slept badly and squinted at fine type; stress and eye strain were common, plausible explanations. His first examination showed no obvious emergency. Dr. He wrote 'tension from overtime' on his time card and sent him back with a follow-up date after the shipment.\n\nThe next Monday, Xiaopu returned before lunch. The ache had eased outdoors during the cart delivery and returned within half an hour of entering the print room. A young packer beside him had begun rubbing her temples; the foreman called it contagious complaining. Dr. He added another familiar label—solvent smell, missed breakfast, poor sleep—and reached for the door. Then she stopped and copied the week's pattern onto six candidate cards. Some explanations were likely and harmless; others were less common but carried greater consequences if missed.\n\nShe asked what changed on Sundays, who else felt unwell, where symptoms began, and whether they followed one machine, task, meal, or room. She repeated the assessments, reviewed warning features, and requested workplace measurements rather than ordering every medical test she could name. The cards shifted as evidence arrived. Migraine, vision strain, dehydration, and anxiety remained possible for an individual, but they did not easily explain several workers improving outside the same room. A recently installed gas heater with a partly blocked flue became a stronger and more dangerous candidate.\n\nThe manager refused to stop production on the final night. He reminded Dr. He that an environmental alarm would void her clinic contract and cost Xiaopu's entire crew its bonus. Then the packer became confused beside the paper cutter. Dr. He started to write 'stress' for a second person, tore the page in half, and stopped the line. She activated the site's emergency and occupational-safety response and sent affected workers for individualized assessment. Testing of the room confirmed a harmful combustion-gas exposure; the responsible teams took over the building response and each person's care.\n\nThe shipment left four days late. Xiaopu lost his bonus, Dr. He's contract went to review, and the manager posted her name beside the delay. The workers recovered, and the heater remained sealed until the fault was corrected and verified. Dr. He kept Xiaopu's old time card clipped to the candidate cards, with the evidence that raised or lowered each one. Its Sunday squares were blank. After the shutdown, four weekday squares were blank too, their clean paper sharper than the six dark punches before them.",
