    "collection": "field-core-fables",
    "storyBody": "Director Han promised the port board that a new campaign would reveal a rare lung disease before the summer inspection. Ten thousand symptom-free dock workers filed through the screening units. The brochure displayed two rounded figures: the test found about nine of every ten affected people and correctly cleared almost ninety-nine of every hundred unaffected people. When 120 red slips arrived, Han saw proof of success. He booked the assembly hall, drafted 120 warning letters, and announced that 120 ill workers had been found before anyone collapsed in a warehouse.\n\nDr. Su, who ran the confirmatory clinic, asked how common the condition had been before screening. Han pointed again to the brochure. 'Ninety percent and about ninety-five percent are excellent.' The first frightened workers filled her corridor; several had already told their families they were ill. Many follow-up assessments did not confirm the condition. Han called that an encouraging surprise, but by the third day the queue was delaying people who had come with symptoms for other reasons. The red slips had created fear and opportunity costs before anyone had interpreted what they meant.\n\nDr. Su cleared a warehouse table and represented the ten thousand workers with counters. Local data suggested that roughly one tenth of one percent of this low-risk workforce—about ten people—would have the condition before testing. At ninety-percent sensitivity, around nine would screen positive. The remaining 9,990 were unaffected. A specificity rounded to 98.9 percent still allowed about 111 false positives in this illustrative set. Nine and 111 produced the 120 red slips on Han's desk.\n\nHan counted the piles twice. The test had not become defective, and the nine were not unimportant. But a red result in this setting did not give its holder a ninety-percent probability of disease. The starting frequency determined how many affected and unaffected people entered the test, while sensitivity and specificity determined how those groups were sorted. Predictive meaning emerged only after all three were combined, with sampling uncertainty and clinical context kept visible.\n\nThe board met that afternoon. Han could hide behind the rounded brochure and continue the publicity; the campaign budget would be renewed while Dr. Su's clinic absorbed the queue. Instead he withdrew the first letters, paid overtime to contact all 120 workers with corrected language and appropriate follow-up arrangements, and surrendered his inspection bonus. The celebratory report was delayed. People who needed further assessment still received it, but nobody was told that a screening result had already settled the diagnosis.\n\nAt the next meeting Han carried no banner. He brought two glass jars: one held nine dark-red counters, the other 111 pale-red counters. The chair asked how a useful test had produced such a lopsided pair. Han began with ten thousand people and one tenth of one percent, not with the two impressive percentages. Afterward the jars remained in the screening office. The larger one stood beside the packet of 120 red slips, each now stamped with one additional word: 'screen.'",
