    "storyBody": "Ren Kai came to counselling after drinking contributed to missing his daughter's school performance. His family wanted an immediate promise of abstinence. The first worker listed health, money, and family consequences, then pressed him to agree. Ren had arrived ashamed; by the end he was defending the relaxation and friendship he associated with drinking. Change sounded entirely like the counsellor's argument. The missed performance ticket stayed in Ren's wallet, creased from being shown during the argument at home. His daughter had stopped telling him rehearsal times because she expected another absence.\n\nA second counsellor did not debate or pretend there was no risk. She asked what Ren valued about drinking and what no longer fitted the life he wanted, reflecting both sides without ridicule. With permission, she offered brief relevant information and asked what he made of it. Ren named his own reasons: remembering weekends, arguing less at home, and attending the next performance. Ren first answered with short jokes. The counsellor reflected the jokes and the shame beneath them without claiming to know him better than he knew himself. Silence lasted long enough for him to add the next sentence.\n\nHis confidence remained low. The referral service wanted a signed promise that day, but the counsellor left its plan box empty and asked Ren about a past occasion when change had been possible, what had supported it, and one small action he was willing to try. His answer looked unimpressive on the form, yet the language and ownership were his. The blank box cost the service its rapid-completion bonus. When the counsellor refused to manufacture a larger promise, the manager removed the case from the quick-success report.\n\nThey agreed on Ren's plan and follow-up, including how to review difficulty without turning a lapse into either catastrophe or permission to abandon the goal. The counsellor did not manipulate him, make guarantees, or substitute conversation for indicated healthcare or practical housing, financial, and family support. Immediate safety or child-protection duties would still require appropriate action; autonomy was not a reason to delay them. Ren chose who could be involved in ordinary follow-up, but the limits of confidentiality and any relevant protection duties were explained before planning. No one promised that motivation alone would remove dependence, withdrawal risk, or social pressure.\n\nOn the evening of the next school performance, friends invited Ren to the pub. He stood under its sign, read the message, and touched the unused ticket from the previous performance in his wallet. Then he put the phone away and walked toward the school. He sent the counsellor no photograph proving it. At the school door, his daughter saw the old ticket when he opened his wallet for the new one. She took his hand without asking what the counsellor had said.",
